this is a second thought video made  possible by Skillshare who kindly chose  to sponsor this episode or was it  predetermined either way be one of the  first 1,000 people to sign up for  Skillshare using the link below and get  your first two months for just 99 cents  we humans are an interesting species  capable of understanding incredibly  complex ideas about the laws of the  universe yet somehow thinking we operate  outside those laws we're talking as you  probably already gathered about the  notion of free will the idea that I can  do something simply because I want to do  it it makes sense on the surface I  certainly feel like I made the decision  to sleep in this morning but is that  really the case there are a few  different schools of thought we'll start  with what's called libertarian free will  libertarians not to be confused with  political libertarians believe that free  will is incompatible with a  deterministic universe and that agents  have free will therefore determinism the  idea that everything has a past cause  must be false this idea is pretty  appealing to most people because we like  to imagine that everything we do is a  conscious decision that we make go to  class you made that decision skip class  also your decision every action you take  is an action initiated by you and your  conscious brain decided upon out of many  possible options it makes sense right at  any moment you could choose to throw  your phone across the room or raise your  hands in the air or perform any other  action available to you that idea of  having a choice is critical to both  libertarian free will and determinism  this is the part where I make a joke  about both political libertarianism and  libertarian free will not being grounded  in reality not really just kidding at  least about politics because while many  of us would probably say we feel free to  make whatever decisions we choose we  would also probably agree that  everything that happens in the natural  world has a cause and effect if you saw  football flying through the air you  would assume it had been thrown or  kicked this is the underlying belief in  the school of thought known as  determinism the idea that everything  that happens has been caused by past  events making any other option  impossible in other words there is no  choice in the matter if you held a gun  to my head and told me to do something  would you consider my action to be made  freely probably not the gun in this  analogy can be anything past experiences  your genetics a phobia  social norms or any combination of  factors leading you to choose your  action or rather take that action since  it wouldn't really be a free choice and  that's where we run into our problem we  can't really hold both of these beliefs  at the same time or can we this is where  a third school of thought comes in  compatibilism is the idea that free will  and determinism are not mutually  exclusive and defines free will as a  freedom to act according to your own  motives without arbitrary interference  from others one example of this is in  courts of law when a judge has to make a  decision about whether an individual  acted of their own free will under a  specific set of circumstances it's  assumed that the person on trial could  have acted differently than they did  which is why they're on trial in the  first place the basic idea of  compatibilism is that a person may be  free to act according to their own  motives but those motives are determined  one famous quote claims man can do what  he wills but he cannot will what he  wills this is evidenced by an experiment  by Benjamin Libet in which he hooked up  subjects to an EEG and asked them to  perform a simple action like pushing a  button as soon as they were aware of the  wish or urged to act the results showed  that there was always a period of time  preceding the subjects conscious  decision to act that indicated the brain  had already made an unconscious decision  as of 2008 further studies had shown  that the upcoming outcome of such a test  could be found in brain activity up to 7  seconds before the subject was aware of  a conscious urge to perform the action  but let's go back to the court of law if  we truly are subject to these  unconscious pre decisions can we be held  morally responsible for our actions one  prime example of this dilemma can be  found in the case of Charles Whitman a  sniper who killed 16 people and wounded  31 others from the top of a tower at the  University of Texas in Austin  Whitman left a note saying he didn't  understand why he was committing these  horrible crimes and to perform an  autopsy to figure out what was wrong  with him after he died  sure enough the autopsy revealed a tumor  that many suspected to have pressed  against the portion of his brain that  controlled his emotions and led to his  violent actions in this case Whitman was  not forced by any other person to commit  violent crimes but neither was he truly  acting of his own unimpaired volition he  could not will what he willed what do we  do with cases like this extreme medical  cases aside most compatibilist suggest  that we should be able to  take credit for our good actions and  blame for our bad ones as long as a  person is free from outside coercion  they have freedom of action the highest  degree of free will conceivable under  compatibilism whether we are aware of it  or not we are all subject to these  unconscious drivers silently guiding us  to take the path we choose if you wake  up and decide you're going to have ice  cream for breakfast that idea didn't  just come out of nowhere your brain had  made that decision long before you were  aware of it so what do we do if we're  all just biological robots driven by  unknown causes  what does that mean for Humanity maybe  nothing the world will continue as it  always has people making decisions and  taking actions that they feel are their  own ideas and maybe that's all we need